        "id": 1122423
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-20T14:58:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "An expression will either be an unresolved string expression that contains strings, lists, and operations or it will be a resolved expression that contains datums, lists, and operations. Many of the structs have the fields str_expr and datum_expr, when the AST is built the str_expr is filled and after it is resolved the datum_expr is filled.\n\nIf you are writing the AST out before resolving, then you should use the str_exprs and you won\u0027t see any datums.",
      "revId": "6f71db2e8668b68b9788ea4907fd3977af56fd09",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "30b79fb7_fc090d8b",
        "filename": "libsepol/cil/src/cil_write_ast.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 99,
      "author": {
        "id": 1026058
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-20T21:44:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "At the moment, I\u0027m writing this out before cil_resolve_ast(), but I\u0027d like for it to work in both cases (and also before/after fqn_qualify() and post_process()).  My understanding is that the str_expr elements of the various cil_* structs still stick around, so the approach for the nodes I\u0027ve written so far should work in both cases. I\u0027m taking your response to mean that if I keep this case, then this should work for both before and after name resolution, is that correct?\n\nThe resolution phase goes over many passes, most of which I\u0027ve not concerned myself with yet since AOSP policy doesn\u0027t use a lot of the more advanced CIL features like namespacing and the different inheritance rules, but to do this properly and eventually upstream it, those\u0027ll need to be work too.",
      "parentUuid": "b0746fbe_36133666",
      "revId": "6f71db2e8668b68b9788ea4907fd3977af56fd09",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "30b79fb7_ffaa5a23",
        "filename": "libsepol/cil/src/cil_write_ast.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 99,
      "author": {
        "id": 1122423
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-09-21T15:19:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes the str_expr elements stay around so if you want just the AST, then it will still work after the other phases.\n\nThe AST will change however. For blockinherits the block is copied under it, for calls the macro is copied under it. For both of these you just need to stop following the tree when you come to the blockinherit or call.\n\nMore difficult are the cases for tunableif and in. During the resolve stage tunableifs are resolved and either the true or false block is copied over the tunableif. for in statements the in block is copied to the block referred to. For in blocks (which is just a way of saying \"I want these rules in the block named\") the rules in the in block are copied to the named block. The AST will be different in these cases. I don\u0027t think it will be a big deal though.\n\nYou only need to start using the datums if you want to see what everything looks like after macro calls are resolved and named blocks removed (like if you want to print a policy.conf).",
      "parentUuid": "30b79fb7_fc090d8b",
      "revId": "6f71db2e8668b68b9788ea4907fd3977af56fd09",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
